Apple today introduced the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/dynamic-island/">Dynamic Island</a> to the Mac for the first time with its <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/28/apple-new-macbook-pro/">new 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models</a>, bringing Live Activities, Spotlight, and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI together in an interactive area of the display.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Apple-MacBook-Pro-with-PROLED-XDR-Display-Dynamic-Island-Apple-Music-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1440" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1074027" /><br/>
The &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; first launched on the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2022/09/07/APPLE-UNVEILS-IPHONE-14-PRO/">iPhone 14 Pro and iPhone 14 Pro Max</a> in September 2022. It replaced the notch with a smaller TrueDepth camera cutout surrounded by software that expands and changes shape to display alerts, ongoing Live Activities, and controls.<br/>
<br/>
The Mac version carries over the idea of keeping ongoing activities visible and accessible while users work in other apps. Apple says users can do things like answer a phone call, control music playback, and accept an AirDrop request through the interface. The &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; also serves as a location for Spotlight and &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI.<br/>
<br/>
With the new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a>'s touchscreen, actions such as pausing a song can also simply be performed with a tap.<br/>
<br/>
While the original iPhone implementation incorporated the TrueDepth camera system used for <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mask-face-id/">Face ID</a>, the new &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; retains <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/touch-id/">Touch ID</a> for authentication and features a 12-megapixel Center Stage camera. The arrival of the &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj; therefore does not bring &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj; to the Mac.<br/>
<br/>
